From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] interconnect: qcom: sm6375: keep the boot-time floors

Do not register icc_sync_state() on this SoC yet.

icc_node_add() gives every node init_avg = init_peak = INT_MAX when the
provider has no get_bw(), which is what keeps the buses at the rate the
RPM handed over at boot. icc_sync_state() then drops those floors once
every provider has probed:

	if (n->init_avg || n->init_peak) {
		n->init_avg = 0;
		n->init_peak = 0;
		aggregate_requests(n);
		p->set(n, n);
	}

That is correct only when every consumer on the SoC declares its
interconnect paths, because from that moment a node's bandwidth is
exactly what its consumers ask for -- and a consumer that asks for
nothing gets nothing. On sm6375 almost nothing declares paths: the
device tree wires up the display, the IPA and (since the previous patch)
uart1, and that is all. Everything else is silently dropped to zero the
instant sync_state runs.

The Bluetooth controller is what noticed. It sits on uart1 at 3.2 Mbps,
and its firmware download starts timing out:

	Bluetooth: hci0: QCA Downloading qca/crbtfw21.tlv
	Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc00 tx timeout
	Bluetooth: hci0: QCA Failed to send TLV segment (-110)

The timing is the tell: the two setups that run during boot always
succeed, because sync_state has not fired yet, and the failures only
start afterwards. Bisecting boot images agrees -- an image whose kernel
predates the provider being built in passes 150 controller resets out of
150, and every image after it fails within one or two.

Keeping the floors gives up bus scaling, which this port has never
actually had: until the provider was built in it never bound at all, so
the RPM's boot-time vote was what everything ran on. Nothing regresses;
the power saving simply stays unclaimed.

Drop this patch once every consumer that matters declares its paths.
Adding paths one consumer at a time does not work as an interim step:
uart1 was given its own in the previous patch and Bluetooth still fails,
because whatever else the transfer depends on is still at zero.

--- a/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sm6375.c
+++ b/drivers/interconnect/qcom/sm6375.c
@@ -1780,7 +1780,14 @@
 	.driver = {
 		.name = "qnoc-sm6375",
 		.of_match_table = sm6375_noc_of_match,
-		.sync_state = icc_sync_state,
+		/*
+		 * No .sync_state on purpose: it would drop the INT_MAX floors
+		 * icc_node_add() installs, and on this SoC almost no consumer
+		 * declares interconnect paths yet, so those nodes would go to
+		 * zero. That starves the QUP the Bluetooth UART runs on, which
+		 * is how this was found. Restore it once the device tree wires
+		 * up the consumers -- see the commit message.
+		 */
 	},
 };
 module_platform_driver(sm6375_noc_driver);
